A||||6||DXR_DAD||Front end bushings????||||5||
Z||000000||DXR_DAD||03-04-2000||01:04 PM||||Is there any company/brand of bushings (rubber) that are better than stock. I am thinking of doing the front end of my 87. It feels ok but 13yrs old it is time. Like I said in my "code blue" post I want to keep this car stock and don't want to spend lots of money, so I don't need poly I just want a nice smooth ride.<P><BR>Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||03-04-2000||04:47 PM||||Likely if you go to the part store you'll get Moog or some other aftermarket.<P>Pretty much all the rubber ones are as good as another. I'd take a Moog part if I can get it. Those are always good.<P>other than racing, I've always been happy with good rubber bushings. For a daily driver car I'd only put polly in certain places. Sway bar bushings, dog bone, and cradle for Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Trainer||03-04-2000||07:54 PM||||Where do you buy the rubber bushings?  I've tried the local parts stores and the Pontiac dealers, and they say rubber is not available.  If someone has a phone number to purchase the bushings I'd very much appreciate a post here.   Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000003||batboy||03-04-2000||08:24 PM||||I looked them up on wrenchhead.com, here is an URL for them.<P> <A HREF="http://www.wrenchead.com:80/wh_mem/resultlist.asp?%5FCAR%5FCODE%5FID=4477&part%5Ftype%5Fid=2460&search%5Ftype=3&mscssid=G2ASQN44VWSH2KK50017QKFCCB7Q9NUD" TARGET=_blank>http://www.wrenchead.com:80/wh_mem/resultlist.asp?%5FCAR%5FCODE%5FID=4477&part%5Ftype%5Fid=2460&search%5Ftype=3&mscssid=G2ASQN44VWSH2KK50017QKFCCB7Q9NUD</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||03-04-2000||08:47 PM||||There are only 3 types of bushings for control arms.<P>1. OEM type. that means a vulcanized compressed rubber item 99%+ percent of the time for U.S. cars.<P>2. Polyurethane. nearlly all these are aftermarket. Almost no OEM product uses it.<P>3. Hard bearing. this can be a number of combinations. Brass. needle bearings. ball bearings. etc.<P>The parts stores don't list what a bushing is made from most of the time for OEM applications. That means for Fiero you will likely get e rubber part that is pressed into the A-arm.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||03-06-2000||05:02 PM||||While you're doing the bushings,make sure and have a good shop press them in,the control arms are pretty easily ruined by a novice press operator.The rear control arms are a good place to put poly bushings also,there's movement there even under normal driving with the stock,rubber bushings.<BR>Unless you have a six cylinder,keep the poly away from the motor on a street car;new rubber is just fine,and won't buzz out the car with vibration.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||03-06-2000||09:02 PM||||I have used McQuay-Norris rubber bushings in my 85SE with very good results.  I am now rebuilding the front end in my daughter's 87 Coupe.  I was able to get them from AutoZone, but they don't seem to be stocking them any more.  Try carparts.com or wrenchead.com  FB371 for the lower control arm, FB395 for the upper arm in the 84-87's.||||reg||1
